{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 149,
    "dDurationMs": 3181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has nothing to do with physics, but you\nknow how sometimes kitschy shops have signs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3330,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where \"the\" is spelled with a \"Y\"?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5100,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ever wonder where that came from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6640,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ancient runes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7970,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When people started writing in old English,\nthe sounds [þ] and [ð] were represented"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11700,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a symbol called thorn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13410,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As in, \"thorn-O-R-N\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15650,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The use of a digraph, that is, two separate\nletters, to represent thorn was only introduced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19830,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the French and their crazy \"way-more-letters-than-necessary-spellings\"\nafter the Norman invasion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24419,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So English scribes started using \"T-H\" to\nspell newfangled French words like \"theatre\","
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28710,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Esther,\" and \"neanderthal\", and in the meanwhile\nbecame sloppier and gradually stopped writing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32660,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the top part of thorn out of laziness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34790,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when printing presses started showing up\nin England in the late 15th century and their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37810,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "European typefaces didn't have stamps for\nthe letter thorn, creative printers decided"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41840,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that \"Y\" was close enough, plus it saved them\none letter's worth of valuable space over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45590,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more state-of-the-art \"T-H\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47130,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thus, they would abbrev."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48860,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"the\" as Y-E, \"that\" as Y-T, \"this\" as Y-S,\nand so on, like this excerpt from the mayflower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55250,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compact, or \"ye olde philadelphia mint.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57790,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's where \"the olde shoppe\" spelling\ncomes from… so when you hear people pronounce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Y-E as \"yee\", you might want to remind them\nthat \"ye\" in old english means \"you all\"…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66000,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and as cool as it is, I'm not sure \"y'all\nold shop\" conveys the \"Merry Old England\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73280,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibe they're going for."
    } ]
  } ]
}
